`getElementById` finds the first matching ID in tree order (Collector.findFirst with pre-order traversal) — that satisfies "duplicate ids → first form wins" if we search from the document root.

Now the design. I'll rewrite `FormElement`:

```java
public class FormElement extends Element {
    private final Elements linkedEls = new Elements();
    private static final Evaluator submittable = ...; // FormSubmitTags
    private static final Evaluator formListed = Selector.evaluatorOf("button, fieldset, input, keygen, object, output, select, textarea");

    public Elements elements() {
        // live computation
        Elements els = select(formListed); // current descendants, doc order
        // add parse-linked controls not currently descendants (foster-parented etc.), if not rehomed
        for (Element linkedEl : linkedEls) {
            if (linkedEl.ownerDocument() != null && !els.contains(linkedEl)
                && !linkedEl.hasAttr("form") && linkedEl.closest("form") == null) {
                els.add(linkedEl);
            }
        }
        // add controls elsewhere in the document that point to this form via the form attribute
        String id = id(); // attr("id")
        if (!id.isEmpty()) {
            Document doc = ownerDocument();
            if (doc != null) {
                for (Element el : doc.select(formListed)) {
                    if (!els.contains(el) && el.attr("form").equals(id) && resolvesTo(el, this))
                        els.add(el);
                }
            }
        }
        // sort into document order
        ...
    }
