Scenario: detached form F (own tree), linkedEls contains control X in document D (added via addElement while attached elsewhere... e.g., user creates FormElement, calls addElement(controlInDoc)). elements(): doc == null → candidates = select(listed) on F; linkedEls loop: `doc == null ||` → true → isAssociated(X): no form attr; ancestor walk — X's ancestors don't include F → orphan fallback: linkedEls.contains(X) ✓; marker — addElement marked X's parent (in doc D) → X still under that parent → associated → members = [F's descendants..., X]. Sort: X in tree D, descendants in tree F → cross-tree compare → root mismatch → siblingIndex both 0 → 0. Two descendants A < B in F's tree, X compares 0 to both → cmp(A,X)=0, cmp(X,B)=0, cmp(A,B)=-1 → violates transitivity → TimSort MAY throw (only for lists > 32 elements with specific patterns; small lists use binary sort which doesn't check). Risky but very edge. The task says "任何输入都不得抛出异常" — let me make the comparator total and safe: if roots differ, order by System.identityHashCode(root). That's deterministic and transitive.

Let me update documentOrder:

```java
private static int documentOrder(Node a, Node b) {
    if (a == b) return 0;
    ArrayList<Node> chainA = ancestorChain(a);
    ArrayList<Node> chainB = ancestorChain(b);
    Node rootA = chainA.get(0);
    Node rootB = chainB.get(0);
    if (rootA != rootB) // different trees: stable, deterministic tiebreak
        return Integer.compare(System.identityHashCode(rootA), System.identityHashCode(rootB));
    int shared = Math.min(chainA.size(), chainB.size());
    for (int i = 1; i < shared; i++) { // start at 1; roots are equal
        Node na = chainA.get(i);
        Node nb = chainB.get(i);
        if (na != nb)
            return Integer.compare(na.siblingIndex(), nb.siblingIndex());
    }
    return Integer.compare(chainA.size(), chainB.size());
}
